<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The privacy manifest for the AbridgeNotes.xcframework the partner embeds. Copied
  to the root of each slice's framework bundle, which is where Apple reads it.

  Every declaration below is derived from a call site in the shipped binary, which
  is this framework's own sources plus AbridgeRecorderCore: the pod is statically
  merged in (Podfile, deliberately no `use_frameworks!`), so Apple's scan of this
  binary sees core's symbols, and the pod as consumed here carries no manifest of
  its own to carry them: its podspec ships `Sources/**/*.swift` with no resources,
  so these declarations are this file's to make.
  AbridgeRecorderCore's vendored OpusEncoder is merged in too and reaches no
  required-reason API — `nm -u` over both libOpusEncoder.a slices finds no
  stat/fstat/getattrlist/statfs symbol.

  Both categories are declared because the symbol is in the binary, which is
  what Apple's submission check scans — not because a runtime path is known to
  reach it. The call site that earns each, and the reason chosen:

    NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp / C617.1
      AbridgeRecorderCore EncounterRecordingRepository.loadAll reads the
      creation date of each persisted recording to sort them oldest-first,
      through both keys Apple lists for this category —
      URLResourceKey.creationDateKey, passed to contentsOfDirectory, and
      FileAttributeKey.creationDate, read out of attributesOfItem. (The
      FileManager methods themselves are not on Apple's list; the keys are.)
      This runs on every configure, through the SDK's restoration path, so it is
      live usage rather than a declaration made for a symbol nothing reaches.
      C617.1 is "metadata of files inside the app container", which these are:
      the audio recordings AbridgeRecorderCore persists under the host's
      Documents directory. (Not the SDK's own encounter records, which live
      under Application Support and are read without any timestamp key.)

    NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime / 35F9.1
      AbridgeRecorderCore EncounterRecordingManager.emitAudioLevel reads
      ProcessInfo.systemUptime as the clock its 100ms audio-level throttle
      measures against — 35F9.1's "time that has elapsed between events that
      occurred within the app". 35F9.1 also forbids sending the value
      off-device, which holds absolutely here: emitAudioLevel returns at its
      `guard enableAudioLevelMetering` before the read, because the SDK is the
      one caller that builds the manager with metering off, and no SDK request
      body or delegate callback carries an audio level or a timestamp either way.
      8FFB.1 would be the reason for stamping absolute event times; that is not
      what this does.

  The other three categories are absent because no call site exists: no
  UserDefaults, no disk-space (volumeAvailableCapacity / statfs) and no
  active-keyboard (UITextInputMode.activeInputModes) reference in either source
  tree, and none of those symbols in the built binary.

  NSPrivacyTracking is false and NSPrivacyTrackingDomains empty because the
  framework links no analytics, crash-reporting or advertising SDK — its imports
  are Apple frameworks plus the local OpusEncoder, and the delivery build asserts
  that no such library is linked in — and its only egress is the Abridge API host
  for the selected ServerEnvironment: api-dev / api-test / api.abridge.care.

  NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is deliberately absent rather than empty. An empty
  array is the affirmative claim that nothing is collected, which would be wrong:
  the framework uploads encounter audio and the patient and provider identifiers
  the host supplies. How those map onto Apple's data types for an embedding app's
  disclosure is not settled in this file, and this file does not assert it.
  Section 4 of the integration guide, under "What the SDK sends off-device", lists
  every value the SDK transmits and on which request — the input that disclosure
  needs.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
